    "label": "thm:instantaneous",
    "number": "Theorem 1",
    "quote": "Fix $\\epsilon \\in \\big(0,\\frac{1}{2}\\big)$, $B > 0$, and $T \\in \\ZZ$. Suppose there exists an algorithm that, for any $f \\in \\Fc_k(B)$, achieves an average simple regret $\\EE[r^{(T)}] \\le \\epsilon$ after $T$ rounds. Then, provided that $\\frac{\\epsilon}{B}$ is sufficiently small, we have the following: For $k = \\kSE$, it is necessary that $T = \\Omega\\bigg( \\frac{\\sigma^2}{\\epsilon^2} \\Big(\\log\\frac{B}{\\epsilon}\\Big)^{d/2} \\bigg)$."
   },
   "confidence": "high",
   "notes": "Number of rounds needed for expected simple regret <= epsilon. Section 5.4 remarks a constant-probability version holds with same scaling (not separately recorded)."
